<python>
cs_content_header = "Lecture 1: Python Basics"
</python>

<h1>Python Basics: Variables, Data Types, and First Programs</h1>

<h2>What is Programming?</h2>
<p><strong>Programming</strong> is the process of writing instructions that a computer can execute to solve a problem or perform a task. A computer is extremely fast and precise — but it does exactly what you tell it to do, nothing more and nothing less. Writing good programs means giving precise, logical instructions.</p>

<div class="highlight">
  <strong>Why Python?</strong> Python is one of the most popular programming languages in the world. It has a clean, readable syntax that closely resembles English, making it ideal for beginners. It is also extremely powerful — used by Google, Netflix, NASA, and researchers in AI and data science worldwide.
</div>

<h2>Installing Python</h2>
<p>To run Python programs on your own computer:</p>
<ol>
  <li>Visit <a href="https://www.python.org/downloads/">python.org/downloads</a> and download Python 3 (any version 3.8 or above).</li>
  <li>Install a code editor: <strong>VS Code</strong> (from <a href="https://code.visualstudio.com">code.visualstudio.com</a>) is recommended and free.</li>
  <li>Or use an online editor such as <a href="https://replit.com">replit.com</a> or <a href="https://colab.research.google.com">Google Colab</a> — no installation needed.</li>
</ol>
<p>To run a Python file from the command line: <code>python3 filename.py</code></p>

<h2>Your First Python Program</h2>
<p>Every programmer's first program is "Hello, World!" — a program that simply prints a message to the screen.</p>
<pre><code># This is a comment — Python ignores it
print("Hello, World!")
</code></pre>
<p>Output: <code>Hello, World!</code></p>

<p>The <code>print()</code> function displays text on the screen. The text must be enclosed in quotation marks (single <code>'</code> or double <code>"</code> — both work).</p>

<h2>Comments</h2>
<p>A <strong>comment</strong> is a note in your code that Python ignores when running the program. Comments help you and others understand what your code does.</p>
<pre><code># This is a single-line comment

# You can also write comments at the end of a line of code:
print("Hello")  # This prints a greeting
</code></pre>

<h2>Variables</h2>
<p>A <strong>variable</strong> is a named container that stores a value. You create a variable by assigning it a name and a value using the equals sign <code>=</code>.</p>
<pre><code>name = "Chinedu"
age = 20
height = 1.75
is_student = True

print(name)   # Output: Chinedu
print(age)    # Output: 20
</code></pre>

<p><strong>Rules for variable names:</strong></p>
<ul>
  <li>Can contain letters, digits, and underscores</li>
  <li>Must start with a letter or underscore (not a digit)</li>
  <li>Case-sensitive: <code>Age</code> and <code>age</code> are different variables</li>
  <li>Cannot use Python reserved words (keywords) like <code>if</code>, <code>for</code>, <code>while</code>, <code>print</code></li>
  <li>Use descriptive names: <code>student_name</code> is better than <code>x</code></li>
</ul>

<h2>Data Types</h2>
<p>Every value in Python has a <strong>data type</strong> that determines what kind of data it holds and what operations can be performed on it.</p>

<table>
  <tr>
    <th>Data Type</th>
    <th>Python Name</th>
    <th>Description</th>
    <th>Example</th>
  </tr>
  <tr>
    <td>Integer</td>
    <td><code>int</code></td>
    <td>Whole numbers (positive or negative)</td>
    <td><code>age = 21</code></td>
  </tr>
  <tr>
    <td>Float</td>
    <td><code>float</code></td>
    <td>Decimal (fractional) numbers</td>
    <td><code>gpa = 3.85</code></td>
  </tr>
  <tr>
    <td>String</td>
    <td><code>str</code></td>
    <td>Text — sequence of characters in quotes</td>
    <td><code>name = "Blessing"</code></td>
  </tr>
  <tr>
    <td>Boolean</td>
    <td><code>bool</code></td>
    <td>True or False values</td>
    <td><code>passed = True</code></td>
  </tr>
</table>

<p>You can check the type of any value using <code>type()</code>:</p>
<pre><code>x = 42
print(type(x))    # &lt;class 'int'&gt;

y = "hello"
print(type(y))    # &lt;class 'str'&gt;
</code></pre>

<h2>Basic Operators</h2>

<h3>Arithmetic Operators</h3>
<table>
  <tr><th>Operator</th><th>Operation</th><th>Example</th><th>Result</th></tr>
  <tr><td><code>+</code></td><td>Addition</td><td><code>5 + 3</code></td><td>8</td></tr>
  <tr><td><code>-</code></td><td>Subtraction</td><td><code>10 - 4</code></td><td>6</td></tr>
  <tr><td><code>*</code></td><td>Multiplication</td><td><code>3 * 7</code></td><td>21</td></tr>
  <tr><td><code>/</code></td><td>Division (always float)</td><td><code>9 / 2</code></td><td>4.5</td></tr>
  <tr><td><code>//</code></td><td>Floor (integer) division</td><td><code>9 // 2</code></td><td>4</td></tr>
  <tr><td><code>%</code></td><td>Modulus (remainder)</td><td><code>9 % 2</code></td><td>1</td></tr>
  <tr><td><code>**</code></td><td>Exponentiation</td><td><code>2 ** 8</code></td><td>256</td></tr>
</table>

<pre><code>price = 1500
quantity = 4
total = price * quantity
discount = total * 0.1
final = total - discount
print("Final price:", final)   # Output: Final price: 5400.0
</code></pre>

<h2>String Operations</h2>
<p>Strings support special operations:</p>
<pre><code>first = "Covenant"
last = "University"
full = first + " " + last    # Concatenation: "Covenant University"
print(full)

greeting = "Hello " * 3      # Repetition: "Hello Hello Hello "
print(greeting)

name = "Nigeria"
print(len(name))              # Length: 7
print(name.upper())           # NIGERIA
print(name.lower())           # nigeria
print(name[0])                # N  (first character, index 0)
print(name[-1])               # a  (last character)
</code></pre>

<h2>Input and Output</h2>
<p>The <code>input()</code> function reads text typed by the user. It always returns a <strong>string</strong>.</p>
<pre><code>name = input("What is your name? ")
print("Welcome, " + name + "!")

age = int(input("Enter your age: "))   # Convert string to int
year_of_birth = 2024 - age
print("You were born around", year_of_birth)
</code></pre>

<h2>f-strings: Formatted Output</h2>
<p><strong>f-strings</strong> (formatted string literals) are the modern, clean way to include variable values inside strings. Prefix the string with <code>f</code> and put variables inside <code>{}</code>:</p>
<pre><code>name = "Adaeze"
score = 87
print(f"Student: {name}, Score: {score}%")
# Output: Student: Adaeze, Score: 87%
</code></pre>

<h2>A Complete First Program</h2>
<pre><code># Simple grade calculator
print("--- Grade Calculator ---")
student_name = input("Enter student name: ")
score = float(input("Enter score (0-100): "))

if score >= 70:
    grade = "A"
elif score >= 60:
    grade = "B"
elif score >= 50:
    grade = "C"
elif score >= 45:
    grade = "D"
else:
    grade = "F"

print(f"\nStudent: {student_name}")
print(f"Score:   {score}")
print(f"Grade:   {grade}")
</code></pre>

<div class="highlight">
  <strong>Practice Tip:</strong> The best way to learn programming is by doing. Type every code example by hand (don't copy-paste), run it, and then experiment by changing values and seeing what happens. Errors are normal — they are how you learn.
</div>
<div id="live-file-panel" style="display:none;margin:20px 0;padding:16px;border:1px solid #ddd;border-radius:6px;background:#f9f9f9;">
  <strong>Live File</strong>
  <div id="live-file-content" style="margin-top:8px;color:#555;"></div>
</div>
<script>
(function(){
  const parts = window.location.pathname.split('/').filter(Boolean);
  const apiBase = 'http://172.31.184.39:8000';
  let course = '', week = '';
  if(parts.length >= 3 && parts[0] === 'courses'){
    course = parts[1];
    week = parts[2];
  } else if(parts.length >= 2 && parts[0] !== 'courses'){
    course = parts[0];
    week = parts[1];
  } else {
    return;
  }
  course = course.trim();
  week = week.trim();
  if(!course || !week) return;
  const panel = document.getElementById('live-file-panel');
  const content = document.getElementById('live-file-content');
    function renderFile(meta){
    if(!meta){
      panel.style.display = 'none';
      return;
    }
    const url = apiBase + '/live-file/' + course + '/' + week;
    const dlUrl = url + '?download=1';
    const type = meta.content_type || '';
    const safeName = meta.filename || 'file';
    let html = '<div><a href="' + dlUrl + '" style="color:#2572F5;text-decoration:underline;cursor:pointer;">Download ' + safeName + '</a></div>';
    if(type.startsWith('image/')){
      fetch(url).then(r=>r.blob()).then(b=>{
        const el = document.getElementById('live-file-img');
        if(el) el.src = URL.createObjectURL(b);
      });
      html += '<div style="margin-top:10px;"><img id="live-file-img" src="" alt="' + safeName + '" style="max-width:100%;height:auto;border:1px solid #ddd;border-radius:4px;"/></div>';
    } else if(type === 'application/pdf'){
      fetch(url).then(r=>r.blob()).then(b=>{
        const el = document.getElementById('live-file-iframe');
        if(el) el.src = URL.createObjectURL(b);
      });
      html += '<div style="margin-top:10px;"><iframe id="live-file-iframe" src="" style="width:100%;height:480px;border:1px solid #ddd;border-radius:4px;"></iframe></div>';
    } else if(type.startsWith('text/')){
      fetch(url).then(r=>r.blob()).then(b=>{
        const el = document.getElementById('live-file-iframe');
        if(el) el.src = URL.createObjectURL(b);
      });
      html += '<div style="margin-top:10px;"><iframe id="live-file-iframe" src="" style="width:100%;height:320px;border:1px solid #ddd;border-radius:4px;background:#fff;"></iframe></div>';
    }
    content.innerHTML = html;
    panel.style.display = 'block';
  }
  async function load(){
    try{
      const r = await fetch(apiBase + '/admin/live-file/' + course + '/' + week);
      if(!r.ok){renderFile(null);return;}
      const d = await r.json();
      renderFile(d || null);
    }catch{
      renderFile(null);
    }
  }
  load();
  setInterval(load, 15000);
})();
</script>
